Ladybird lists every installed extension, including failed loads, and
updates every open management page when the registry changes. It uses a
browser-owned directory picker and confirms removal with explicit notice
that stored data is deleted. Chromium ExtensionsUI hosts its management
page; Service.setItemEnabled() changes enabled state, and
Service.deleteItem() confirms removal through management.uninstall().
DeveloperPrivateLoadUnpackedFunction::ShowSelectFileDialog() chooses an
existing folder in the browser. Ladybird matches these management and
picker behaviors, but adds a permissions confirmation for unpacked
installs.

Gecko AddonCard.handleEvent() enables or disables through AddonManager,
and asks BrowserAddonUI.promptRemoveExtension() before removal. Its
installTemporaryExtension() uses a picker and reports loading errors
separately. Ladybird shares the enable and removal behavior, but
deliberately keeps unpacked installations across browser restarts, and
combines loading and management on one page. Failed installed entries
remain visible with their directory basename and a retry action.

WebKit leaves the management interface to its embedding application.
WebExtensionContext::setHasAccessToPrivateData() updates private access,
while WebExtensionController::removeData() separately removes storage.
Ladybird likewise keeps private access explicit and preserves storage on
disable, deleting it only on confirmed removal from the profile.
